module TrinityFpgaDdr3MatvecT27;

// Device matvec of issue #64, consumer (B) of #65: y = W x for a ternary matrix W streamed
// from DDR3 one 128-bit bus word per controller clock (the word #62's burst reader delivers:
// bytes 0-7 in `in_lo`, 8-15 in `in_hi`, byte n at bits [8n, 8n+7]) and an int8 vector x
// held on chip. Not yet wired into a board top: the DDR3 integration is the next wave
// (docs/bridge.md, "Device matvec"). tests/test_ddr3_matvec.py checks the functions in C and
// the module in Icarus on the real stage-1 chunk.
//
// Row layout (row-padded): every row starts at a bus word. A row of `cols` trits takes
// wpr = ceil(cols / L) words, L = 64 lanes per word for baseline2 (fmt 0, TMEM codec 0: four
// 2-bit lane codes per byte, earliest lane lowest) and L = 80 for dense5 (fmt 1: byte n holds
// lanes 5n .. 5n+4 as one base-3 code, docs/format.md). Lane codes: 00 = 0, 01 = +1, 10 = -1;
// 11 (baseline2) and dense5 codes 243-255 decode to 0 and count as invalid codes. Lanes at
// and after `cols` in a row's last word are forced to 0 (masked), so padding content never
// reaches an accumulator. A 2,560-column row is 40 baseline2 or 32 dense5 words with no
// padding, so the TMEM payload of whole rows is the row-padded image as it is.
//
// Activations: ten banks act0 .. act9 of 1024 u64 words (block RAM). Entry k of bank b holds
// the activations of columns kL + 8b .. kL + 8b + 7 (byte i = column kL + 8b + i, two's
// complement), so one read of entry k in all ten banks gives the 80 (dense5) or 64
// (baseline2, banks 8 and 9 unused) activations of word k of every row: 80 activations per
// clock, the widest word, so a dense5 word needs no second read and the consumer takes one
// word in every clock in both formats. A row may therefore have at most 1024 words: 65,536
// columns in baseline2, 81,920 in dense5. The write port (act_we, act_entry, act_bank,
// act_data) writes one u64 per clock; the device frame handler that fills it from the host's
// activation frames is the next wave (docs/bridge.md, "Wire protocol extension").
//
// A run: `start` with rows (1 .. 1024), cols (>= 1), fmt and seq, while idle. SETUP computes
// wpr and the lanes of the last word by repeated subtraction (no division; at most 1024
// clocks, before the first word), then the lane mask of the last word. RUN takes every word
// offered while it expects one (in_ready is constant true: there is no back-pressure). The
// pipeline behind the input register:
//   S0 word, the flags of its position in the row and the activations of its block (the
//      banks read at the block index of the word being taken, in the same clock)
//   S1 decode: 80 (dense5) or 64 (baseline2) lane codes and a flag per invalid code
//   S2 mask of the last word's padding lanes; per lane a biased term, 8 bits:
//      +1 -> x xor 0x80 (= x + 128), -1 -> x xor 0x7F (= ~x + 128 = -x - 1 + 128),
//      0 -> 0x80 (= 0 + 128), and a bit per -1 lane (the +1 that ~x lacks)
//      (and eight counts of the invalid-code flags, 16 flags each)
//   S3 twenty sums of four terms; five counts of the -1 lanes (16 lanes each); the word's
//      invalid codes (added to the run's count in the next clock)
//   S4 five sums of four; the -1 lanes of the word
//   S4b the sum of the first four of those, and the fifth plus the -1 lanes
//   S5 the word's biased sum plus its -1 lanes (at most 80 * 255 + 80 = 20,480, 16 bits)
//   S6 the word's dot product: that sum - 128 * 80, as 32-bit two's complement
//   S7 the row's accumulator (32-bit two's complement): first word of a row starts at 0
//   S8 the row's result written to the result memory (1024 x u32, block RAM) and folded
//      into the result checksum
// so |y| <= cols * 128 (884,736 for down_proj's 6,912 columns, above 2^19) never wraps below
// 2^31: the accumulators are 32 bits, more than the 21 bits down_proj needs.
// After the last word the run drains the pipeline, then EMIT sends one Y line per row and the
// eleven Z lines through the line emitter (fpga_line_emitter.t27: tag, a, b = (check << 32)
// | v, check the low byte of the CRC-32 of tag, a[4], v[4], as fpga_uart_loader.t27):
//   Y  a = (seq << 24) | row      v = y[row] (32-bit two's complement)
//   Z  a = (seq << 24) | (11 << 16) | index, v = counter:
//      0 status (0 ran, 1 refused: rows or cols out of range, fmt not 0 or 1)
//      1 rows done            2 words per row (wpr)     3 words taken
//      4 cycles: clocks from the first word taken to the last, inclusive
//      5 idle clocks: clocks of that span with no word offered (the bus left the consumer
//        waiting: starvation, the memory-bound signal of #65)
//      6 latency: clocks from the end of SETUP to the first word
//      7 invalid codes (dense5 codes 243-255 in any byte, baseline2 11 lanes in any lane)
//      8 stray words: words offered while no word was expected (before RUN, after the last
//        word of the run, during EMIT)
//      9 consumer stalls: words offered while in_ready was low; in_ready is constant true,
//        so this is 0 by construction and measures nothing
//      10 result checksum: c = rotl1(c) xor y[row] over the rows in order, 32 bits, at the
//        write into the result memory; the host recomputes it from the Y lines it received,
//        so a result memory read that differs from its write shows
// The `a` counters are 32 bits; cycles and idle clocks of one run stay far below 2^32 at
// 83.33 MHz (1024 rows of 1024 words = 2^20 words).
// Constant multiplies are shifts and adds; digit arithmetic is table lookups (the dense5
// tables of fpga_ddr3_reader.t27, checked against bram_trit_codec.t27 in the tests); there is
// no division. Every statement of on_clock reads a variable before the same tick writes it.
const ST_IDLE: u32 = 0;
const ST_SETUP: u32 = 1;
const ST_MASK: u32 = 2;
const ST_RUN: u32 = 3;
const ST_DRAIN: u32 = 4;
const ST_EMIT: u32 = 5;
const E_READ: u32 = 0;
const E_FORM: u32 = 1;
const E_CHECK: u32 = 5;
const E_SEND: u32 = 2;
const E_START: u32 = 3;
const E_WAIT: u32 = 4;
const FMT_B2: u32 = 0;
const FMT_D5: u32 = 1;
const MAX_ROWS: u32 = 1024;
const MAX_WPR: u32 = 1024;
const Z_COUNT: u32 = 11;
const TAG_Y: u32 = 89;
const TAG_Z: u32 = 90;
const BIAS80: u32 = 10240;
const PAIRS: u64 = 6148914691236517205;
const M32: u64 = 4294967295;
const ALL_ONES: u32 = 4294967295;
const CRC_POLY: u32 = 3988292384;

